fix: close the defects found by the release cross-review

An independent read-only review of the three boundaries introduced this round found
concrete holes. Oracle itself could not run (no OPENAI_API_KEY; its private Chrome
profile is not signed in and needs an interactive login), so two independent agents
read the same files and reported findings with line-level evidence. Every item below
was reproduced before being changed.

Trust boundaries
- `manifest.py` anchored name/entry/version with `$`, which in Python also matches
  *before a trailing newline*. `version: |` in YAML yields `"1.2.3\n"`; it passed the
  shape check and `version_key` silently turned it into `1.2.0`, so a blocked release
  installed under a key no blocklist contained. Anchored with `\Z`, switched to
  `fullmatch`, and made `version_key` quarantine malformed remote values under `?`
  instead of letting them collide with a real release. `block_key` now lowercases the
  name too — `"Demo"` in a hand-written blocklist never matched `demo`.
- `index_client.snapshot()` cached the failure snapshot, whose `blocked` map contains
  only local entries. The first timeout or 503 therefore dropped every remote block,
  contradicting the install endpoint's own comment about stale caches. Failed refreshes
  now merge into the last good map; blocks can only be added.
- A missing or corrupt local blocklist was invisible above a log line. Added
  `warnings` to `IndexSnapshot`/`/api/plugins/discover` and a banner in the panel.
- `channel` was read straight from the plugin's own manifest, so any uploaded zip
  could add one line and render as "official, reviewed with the release". The API now
  decides provenance from an explicit in-repo set plus the package path.
- The panel looked blocks up with `${name}@${version}` while the backend uses a
  canonical key — `1.0` in the index showed no warning for a block on `1.0.0`.
  Added `pluginVersions.ts` with the same normalisation, plus unit tests.
- `restart_hint()` interpolated the service name into a command a human pastes into a
  shell. The automatic path passes it as argv and was already safe; the hint now quotes
  plain names and refuses to print a command at all for cmd.exe metacharacters.

Restart path
- `/api/system/restart` was not idempotent: concurrent requests each scheduled an
  exit task. The Windows spawn slept synchronously on the event loop and its Task
  exception was never retrieved, so a failed `schtasks` left the process running after
  the endpoint answered `scheduled: True`. Now guarded, run via `to_thread`, logged.
- `SecurityView` declared "restart complete" on the first healthy `/api/health` —
  which the OLD process answers happily when the restart request was refused. It now
  records the old process's `uptime_seconds` before saving, only accepts a smaller
  uptime (or an observed outage), reports HTTP refusals immediately, bounds each probe
  with AbortController, and invalidates earlier poll loops. A failed `access.reload()`
  no longer shows "已生效" over stale permission data.
- `useAsync` now clears a stale error when a retry starts and stops applying results
  after unmount. A failed login's reason is no longer erased by the 401 handler.

Verified: 1637 passed / 10 skipped, ruff clean, security boundaries 76/76, plugin IPC
65, test gaps strict, doc links, deps, secrets, deploy and desktop gates all pass;
`npm run verify` green (37 tests). Known limitations not addressed here: concurrent
`.env` read-modify-write across different endpoints has no shared lock, and loading an
already-installed blocked version is still reported but not refused.
